The board state has 11 pieces for each player, with column heights \([0, 3, 6, 5, 5, 3, 0]\). Player \(x\) has no immediate winning placement and no open three-in-a-row that can be completed on the present turn. Player \(o\), however, possesses three latent winning cells: \((1,1)\), \((1,5)\) and \((4,6)\). Each of these cells becomes an immediate threat once the single empty square beneath it is filled.

A concrete forcing sequence available to \(o\) is:

- \(o\) drops in column 5 (occupying \((2,5)\)), opening the threat at \((1,5)\);
- \(x\) must block;
- \(o\) drops in column 6 (occupying \((5,6)\)), opening the threat at \((4,6)\);
- \(x\) must block;
- \(o\) drops once more in column 6 (occupying \((3,6)\)).

At that moment two distinct winning cells for \(o\) become simultaneously playable: \((2,6)\) (horizontal) and \((0,3)\) (diagonal). Because \(x\) can block only one of them, \(o\) wins on the following move. The same double threat can be realized by any transposition of the first two forcing moves.

The unique empty cell that participates in this double threat is \((0,3)\). Occupying it on the present turn (column 3) permanently eliminates the diagonal component of the double threat. After the occupation the same sequence produces only a single threat at each step; \(x\) can answer every threat and the position remains defensible. No analogous double-threat sequence exists that would require \(x\) to occupy any other cell on the first move. Consequently every legal first move except column 3 loses by force, while column 3 is the unique non-losing (and therefore optimal) choice.

Final Answer: 3

